<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kołobrzeg</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Kolberg</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kołobrzeg, in German Kolberg, is a port and spa town on the Baltic coast of north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>. Bolesław I made it the seat of one of Poland's first bishoprics in 1000, and from 1248 it belonged to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamien-pomorski">Cammin</a>. Its salt works brought wealth, and its defence against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>'s forces in 1807 became a celebrated episode of Prussian resistance.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kolobrzeg.jpg" alt="Kołobrzeg" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Klugschnacker · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ko%C5%82obrzeg,_Hafen,_Leuchtturm,_f_(2011-07-26)_by_Klugschnacker_in_Wikipedia.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
